// Leases are crash-safe: the file is built under a temporary name, forced and
// atomically renamed into place, so a lease is either wholly present or
// absent. A holder killed mid-operation stops renewing its lease; once the
// expiry passes the next backup, incremental export, merge, restore or open
// of the directory treats the lease as dead, removes it, and takes over — the
// chain underneath is either the old chain or one complete new chain, so
// recovery never needs a manual step and cannot deadlock. A well-formed lease
// that has not reached its expiry is never taken over: the contender fails
// with fs.ErrInvalid and changes nothing. An unparseable lease cannot be a
// live holder (a live holder only ever installs a complete file), so it is
// treated as dead debris and reclaimed rather than wedging the chain.
